  │ [38;5;153mui/*[39m          │ Task list, watch toggle, bell + inbox panel, toast host             │ all of the above          │
  └───────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘

  Data shapes:

  Task          { id, title, status, dueAt|null, updatedAt }
  Event         { seq, type: "status"|"due-date"|"due"|"overdue",
                  taskId, before, after, at, origin: tabId }
  Subscription  { id, kind: "task", taskId, createdAt }
  Notification  { id, eventSeq, taskId, type, at, read: false }

  Three things this shape buys deliberately:

  - [1m[38;5;153mkind[39m on Subscription[0m is the extension point you approved. [38;5;153m"rule"[39m slots in by adding a matcher branch; [38;5;153mevents[39m,
    [38;5;153mnotifications[39m, and the UI never change.
  - [1m[38;5;153morigin: tabId[0m is how we honor "don't notify me about my own action in this tab" without losing the event — the other
    tab still gets it. Self-notification becomes a one-line filter, not a structural decision.
  - [1m[38;5;153meventSeq[39m on Notification[0m makes materialization idempotent. Two tabs racing the same event produce the same key, so a
    duplicate is a no-op rather than a double toast.

  [1mTooling[0m — nothing is configured yet, and this is the cheapest moment to set it up. My suggestion: a test runner plus
  lint/format from the start (the due-date and dedupe logic genuinely need tests), and no build step or framework —
  vanilla ES modules keep the static page static and the modules directly unit-testable.
